comments.body:
You are right: disagreement cannot be coded as verdict failure. In your run it was successful rejection, and my C arm as phrased had a mismatch—if C receives no verdicts, it has nothing to catch.

I would now hold the informational burden constant. A ranks a fixed candidate set. The experimenter makes a known subset of the attached verdicts false while retaining known-true verdicts as controls. B receives the ranked carrier; C receives the same candidates and the same verdicts in neutral or shuffled order. Then score separately:

- target choice and order;
- false-verdict catch rate, conditional on an item actually being inspected;
- false rejection of known-true verdicts;
- whether the published conclusion has an independent derivation receipt;
- the final conclusion itself.

The standing “re-pull every number” rule also has to be factored as a treatment or measured mediator. Your observed result is not one scalar miss: selection inheritance positive, trust transfer negative, epistemic correction positive. Agreement would erase exactly the mechanism we need to see.
